/* v4 learner-facing rewrites: Groups (12/12) */
Object.assign(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY||(window.CANON_V4_MASTERY={}),{
'g.map':{title:'The Bible shelf is organized, but not by one single rule',dek:'Shelf groups help you navigate; genre tells you how a passage works. Those are related ideas, not the same thing.',body:['A Bible is a library, so its books are arranged into recognizable neighborhoods. In the Protestant Old Testament, readers commonly speak of Law, History, Poetry/Wisdom, Major Prophets, and Minor Prophets. In the New Testament, the shelf moves through Gospels/Acts, Pauline letters, General letters, and Revelation.','Those shelf labels are practical, but they do not mean every book in a group uses one literary form. Exodus contains narrative and law. Daniel contains court stories and symbolic visions. Psalms contains many kinds of poetry. A letter can quote hymns, tell stories, argue, command, and pray.','The safest habit is two-step orientation: first use the shelf group to locate the book, then identify the actual literary form of the passage you are reading. That prevents “group” from becoming a shortcut that flattens the text.'],plain:'Use groups to find the neighborhood. Use genre to decide how to read the particular passage.',visual:{type:'stack',title:'Two labels doing different jobs',layers:[{title:'Shelf group',text:'Where the book sits in this edition.'},{title:'Book',text:'The larger work you are inside.'},{title:'Passage genre',text:'Narrative, poetry, law, letter, vision, proverb, and more.'},{title:'Reading strategy',text:'Questions that fit the passage’s form and purpose.'}]},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Group or genre?',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['“Major Prophets” is a shelf group.','A book inside one shelf group can contain several genres.','Every verse in a prophetic book is a prediction.','Shelf group alone determines how literally every sentence should be read.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['Organizational category is not the same thing as literary form.'],why:'Shelf group helps location; passage genre guides interpretation.'}},
'g.law':{title:'How to read Torah without turning it into a random rule list',dek:'The Law/Pentateuch mixes story, covenant instruction, ritual, genealogy, poetry, and speeches.',body:['Genesis through Deuteronomy is often called “Law,” but large portions are narrative. The books tell creation stories, family stories, liberation, wilderness journeys, rebellions, speeches, and covenant ceremonies alongside commandments and ritual instructions.','When you reach a law, ask where it sits in the covenant story, who receives it, what social or ritual problem it addresses, and whether later biblical writers reuse or reinterpret it. Do not assume every ancient civil or ritual instruction transfers unchanged into a modern setting.','Christians also disagree about how different kinds of Torah relate to Christian life. Responsible reading therefore starts with what the instruction meant inside Israel’s covenant before moving to theological or ethical application.'],plain:'Torah is a story-shaped covenant library, not a spreadsheet of timeless rules.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'Two things Torah is doing at once',columns:[{title:'Narrating a people’s story',items:['Creation and ancestors','Exodus and wilderness','Failure and renewal']},{title:'Forming covenant life',items:['Worship','Justice','Community boundaries','Holiness']}],shared:['Story gives the laws a setting']},challenge:{kind:'scenario',title:'Read a law in context',prompt:'Choose the better reading move.',stages:[{prompt:'You encounter an ancient agricultural command. What first?',choices:['Ask what covenant/community purpose it served.','Apply the surface wording mechanically without context.'],correct:0,feedback:['Good: purpose and setting come before modern transfer.','Reconsider: an ancient legal form needs its covenant and social context.']},{prompt:'You want a modern application. What next?',choices:['Identify the enduring concern and test a proportionate application.','Assume ancient Israel and a modern society are identical.'],correct:0,feedback:['Good: preserve the concern without pretending the settings are identical.','Reconsider: application requires analogy, not identity.']}],hints:['Context before transfer.'],why:'Torah is read responsibly by connecting instruction, covenant, and present application without collapsing the historical gap.'}},
'g.hist':{title:'How to read biblical history without assuming every action is approved',dek:'Narrative reports what people do; it often expects you to judge those actions from the larger story rather than copy them.',body:['Joshua through Esther contains long stretches of narrative. Narratives show choices, consequences, conflict, speeches, violence, faithfulness, and failure. A narrator can report an action without endorsing it.','This is especially important in Judges, Samuel, and Kings, where characters who matter greatly can act terribly. Ask what the narrator highlights, what consequences follow, whether the action fits or violates stated covenant values, and how later scenes interpret what happened.','Historical narrative also selects material. It is not a modern camera recording everything. Different biblical histories can retell overlapping events for different purposes, as Chronicles does with material also found in Samuel and Kings.'],plain:'“It happened in the story” is not the same as “the story says you should do it.”',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Read narrative in four moves',nodes:[{title:'What happened?',text:'Observe the action'},{title:'How is it told?',text:'Notice emphasis and consequence'},{title:'What values frame it?',text:'Compare with the book’s wider moral world'},{title:'What should be learned?',text:'Do not copy behavior before interpreting it'}]},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Report or endorsement?',prompt:'Sort the statements.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['A narrator can describe wrongdoing without approving it.','Consequences can help signal narrative evaluation.','Every action performed by a biblical hero is exemplary.','Violence in a story automatically becomes a command for readers.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['Description and prescription are different categories.'],why:'Narrative must be interpreted through framing, consequence, and the wider moral argument.'}},
'g.wis':{title:'How to read wisdom when wise voices disagree',dek:'Proverbs, Job, and Ecclesiastes do not flatten life into one formula; together they teach discernment.',body:['Proverbs often describes patterns that generally make for wise life: diligence tends to produce better outcomes than laziness; reckless speech creates harm; wise counsel matters. A proverb is compact guidance, not a legal guarantee that every righteous person will receive the expected result.','Job challenges the idea that suffering can always be explained as the sufferer’s fault. His friends say many things that sound proverbially reasonable, but the book exposes how badly generalized wisdom can fail when applied without evidence.','Ecclesiastes then presses questions about mortality, achievement, injustice, and the limits of human control. Reading these books together teaches a mature wisdom: patterns matter, exceptions matter, context matters, and humility matters.'],plain:'Proverbs gives patterns. Job tests simplistic use of those patterns. Ecclesiastes tests our confidence that life can be controlled.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'Three wisdom voices in conversation',columns:[{title:'Proverbs',items:['Learn patterns','Practice discernment','Expect actions to have consequences']},{title:'Job + Ecclesiastes',items:['Do not blame every sufferer','Life exceeds simple formulas','Human control is limited']}],shared:['Wisdom requires judgment, not slogans']},challenge:{kind:'compare-board',title:'Which reading fits wisdom literature?',prompt:'Sort the statements.',lanes:['Wisdom','Depends','Misuse'],items:['A proverb describes a general pattern.','Every proverb is an unconditional promise.','Job warns against blaming suffering on hidden sin without evidence.','Ecclesiastes explores the limits of achievement and control.'],answer:[0,2,0,0],hints:['A proverb is usually not a contract guaranteeing an outcome.'],why:'Wisdom literature trains judgment by holding patterns, limits, and lived complexity together.'}},
'g.major':{title:'How to read the Major Prophets as voices inside history',dek:'Isaiah, Jeremiah, Lamentations, Ezekiel, and Daniel use poetry, narrative, vision, lament, and symbolic action—not just future prediction.',body:['Prophetic books speak into political and covenant crises. They address rulers, injustice, worship, foreign powers, impending judgment, exile, and restoration. Prediction is present, but it is only one part of prophetic speech.','A prophetic poem may use hyperbole and imagery. A symbolic vision may not function like a newspaper report. Lamentations mourns catastrophe. Daniel shifts between court narrative and apocalyptic imagery. Identifying the form helps prevent literalizing every image in the same way.','Historical anchors matter because references to Assyria, Babylon, Jerusalem, kings, and exile locate the rhetoric. Ask what the prophet is confronting in that world before jumping directly to a modern nation or event.'],plain:'Prophets speak to real crises using many literary forms. Start with their historical audience before searching for modern predictions.',visual:{type:'timeline',title:'Major Prophets around crisis',points:[{date:'8th c. BC',title:'Isaiah',note:'Judah, Assyria, judgment and hope'},{date:'late 7th–6th c. BC',title:'Jeremiah',note:'Jerusalem’s final crisis'},{date:'586 BC',title:'Lamentations',note:'Poetic grief after destruction'},{date:'exilic',title:'Ezekiel',note:'Visions among deportees'},{date:'exilic/imperial setting',title:'Daniel',note:'Court tales and apocalypse'}]},challenge:{kind:'context-lens',title:'Choose the right prophetic lens',prompt:'Build the reading profile.',fields:['First question','Genre awareness','Historical anchor','Common mistake'],options:[['What crisis is this speech addressing?','Which modern headline looks similar?'],['Poetry and vision use imagery deliberately.','All imagery must be literal in the same way.'],['Assyria/Babylon/exile can orient context.','Shelf order alone dates the prophecy.'],['Jumping to modern fulfillment before original context.','Reading the surrounding passage.']],answer:[0,0,0,0],hints:['Original crisis before modern analogy.'],why:'Prophetic interpretation begins with historical setting, literary form, and rhetorical purpose.'}},
'g.minor':{title:'How to read the Twelve without treating “minor” as unimportant',dek:'The Minor Prophets are shorter, not lesser. Their compact books often demand more context because there is less narrative scaffolding.',body:['“Minor” refers to length. Amos, Hosea, Micah, Jonah, Habakkuk, and the other books in the Twelve can carry enormous theological and ethical weight in very little space.','Because the books are short, readers can easily miss historical clues. Who is the king? Is the northern or southern kingdom in view? Has exile happened yet? Is the speech against Israel, Judah, Edom, Nineveh, or another nation? Those details change interpretation.','The Twelve also vary in form. Jonah is primarily a narrative about a prophet; Habakkuk contains dialogue and prayer; Haggai dates speeches precisely; Zechariah includes visions. One shelf group still contains different literary strategies.'],plain:'Minor means shorter. Read each book for its historical target and literary form instead of assuming all Twelve work the same way.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'The Twelve are not one genre',columns:[{title:'Speech-heavy examples',items:['Amos','Hosea','Micah','Haggai']},{title:'Distinctive forms',items:['Jonah — narrative','Habakkuk — dialogue/prayer','Zechariah — visions']}],shared:['All belong to the Twelve']},challenge:{kind:'match-board',title:'Match book to reading clue',prompt:'Connect the book with the useful clue.',items:['Jonah','Haggai','Habakkuk','Zechariah'],options:['Dated rebuilding-era speeches','Narrative centered on a reluctant prophet','Dialogue/complaint and prayer','Symbolic visions'],answer:[1,0,2,3],hints:['Jonah is the obvious narrative outlier.'],why:'The Twelve share a shelf group but use distinct forms and settings.'}},
'g.gospel':{title:'How to read a Gospel as a portrait, not a stenographic transcript',dek:'Gospels narrate Jesus’ life and teaching while selecting, arranging, and interpreting material for readers.',body:['A Gospel is ancient narrative/biographical writing centered on Jesus. The evangelists include sayings, healings, controversies, parables, travel, meals, trial, death, and resurrection—but they select and arrange them rather than documenting every moment.','Pay attention to placement. A story next to another story can create a comparison. Repeated themes signal an author’s emphasis. An Old Testament quotation can frame an event. A narrator’s aside can tell you how the author wants a scene understood.','Comparing Gospels is useful, but first let each one speak on its own. Harmonizing too quickly can erase the very emphasis a writer created by choosing a distinctive sequence or wording.'],plain:'Read each Gospel as an author’s purposeful portrait of Jesus before combining all four into one blended story.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Read a Gospel scene in context',nodes:[{title:'Scene',text:'What happens?'},{title:'Placement',text:'What comes before and after?'},{title:'Repeated themes',text:'What does this Gospel emphasize?'},{title:'Scriptural echoes',text:'What earlier text is being invoked?'},{title:'Portrait',text:'What claim about Jesus is emerging?'}]},challenge:{kind:'scenario',title:'Compare without erasing',prompt:'Choose the better reading move.',stages:[{prompt:'Two Gospels arrange events differently. What first?',choices:['Read each sequence for its literary purpose.','Assume one writer must be careless.'],correct:0,feedback:['Good: arrangement can carry emphasis.','Reconsider: difference in arrangement needs interpretation before judgment.']},{prompt:'You want a combined chronology. What should remain visible?',choices:['Each Gospel’s distinctive emphasis.','Only the harmonized version.'],correct:0,feedback:['Good: synthesis should not erase the sources.','Reconsider: harmonization can hide authorial choices.']}],hints:['Difference can be meaningful.'],why:'A Gospel is a purposeful narrative portrait, not merely raw transcript data.'}},
'g.paul':{title:'How to read Paul’s letters as one side of a real conversation',dek:'Paul writes into specific church situations. The letters make more sense when you reconstruct the question or conflict he is answering.',body:['Paul’s letters are “occasional,” meaning they arise from occasions: disputes, questions, reports, travel plans, persecution, worship problems, theological confusion, financial collections, leadership, and relationships.','Because we often have only Paul’s side of the exchange, reconstruction must be careful. A repeated phrase may echo an opponent or a slogan from the community. A command may address a very local problem. Historical background can clarify, but it can also become speculation if it outruns the text.','Follow the argument across paragraphs before isolating a command. Ask what problem Paul is solving, what theological reason he gives, and whether the instruction depends on a temporary circumstance or expresses a broader principle.'],plain:'A letter is an answer sent into a real situation. Read enough of the letter to discover the problem before turning one sentence into a universal rule.',visual:{type:'relationship',title:'A letter has two sides even when we only possess one',nodes:[{title:'Paul / co-senders',role:'Writes and argues'},{title:'Local community',role:'Receives the letter amid a real problem'},{title:'Messengers / reports',role:'Carry news and questions'},{title:'Modern reader',role:'Reconstructs cautiously from surviving evidence'}],note:'Background helps only when it is supported.'},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Reconstruct carefully',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['A command in a letter responds to some communicative situation.','The surrounding argument helps explain the command.','We can know every detail of the lost side of the conversation.','One isolated sentence automatically gives the whole letter’s theology.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['We have strong evidence for a situation, not omniscience about it.'],why:'Letters should be interpreted through their argument and occasion without pretending to know missing details.'}},
'g.gen':{title:'How to read the General Epistles without pretending they are one unified genre',dek:'Hebrews, James, Peter, John, and Jude address different audiences in very different voices.',body:['The label “General Epistles” is organizational. Hebrews reads like a sustained theological sermon/exhortation. James resembles wisdom instruction. 1 Peter speaks to communities facing pressure. The Johannine letters focus on truth, love, teaching, and community boundaries. Jude is brief and polemical.','Because the books differ, one reading strategy will not fit them all. In James, compact instruction should be compared across the letter’s wisdom themes. In Hebrews, follow the long argument and its scriptural interpretations. In 1 John, repeated contrasts and circular development matter.','The shared benefit of the group is perspective: early Christian communities beyond the Pauline corpus are wrestling with endurance, ethics, doctrine, leadership, conflict, and identity in their own ways.'],plain:'“General Epistles” tells you where the books sit, not that Hebrews, James, Peter, John, and Jude all write the same kind of text.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'Different voices in one shelf neighborhood',columns:[{title:'Argument / exhortation',items:['Hebrews — sustained theological exhortation','1 Peter — endurance and identity']},{title:'Wisdom / community boundary',items:['James — practical wisdom','1–3 John — truth, love, community','Jude — urgent warning']}],shared:['All address Christian communities beyond the Pauline collection']},challenge:{kind:'match-board',title:'Match the book to the reading strategy',prompt:'Connect each work with a fitting first move.',items:['Hebrews','James','1 John','Jude'],options:['Follow the sustained scriptural argument','Read compact sayings in the letter’s wisdom frame','Track repeated contrasts of truth/love/teaching','Read a short urgent polemic in context'],answer:[0,1,2,3],hints:['Hebrews is the long argument; Jude is the short warning.'],why:'The General Epistles require book-specific strategies rather than one group-wide genre rule.'}},
'g.proph':{title:'How to read Revelation without turning every image into a secret code',dek:'Revelation is apocalypse, prophecy, and circular letter. Its symbols draw heavily on earlier Scripture and speak to communities under pressure.',body:['Revelation opens as a message to seven churches and quickly moves into symbolic visions. Beasts, horns, numbers, cities, cosmic conflict, worship, judgment, and renewed creation belong to an apocalyptic symbolic world that ancient readers were more familiar with than many modern readers are.','Images often echo Daniel, Exodus, Isaiah, Ezekiel, Zechariah, Psalms, and imperial realities. Before mapping a symbol directly onto a modern politician or technology, ask what earlier scriptural imagery and first-century power structures the image evokes.','Christians disagree about how Revelation’s visions relate to past, present, and future events. Those systems can be studied, but the book’s pastoral purpose should remain visible: resist idolatrous power, endure faithfully, worship God, trust divine judgment, and hope for renewed creation.'],plain:'Revelation uses symbolic visions to form faithful communities. Read the symbols through Scripture and first-century context before building a modern prediction chart.',visual:{type:'stack',title:'Four lenses for Revelation',layers:[{title:'Letter',text:'Addressed to seven real churches'},{title:'Prophecy',text:'Calls communities to faithfulness'},{title:'Apocalypse',text:'Uses symbolic visions to unveil reality'},{title:'Scriptural echo',text:'Reuses images from Israel’s Scriptures'}]},challenge:{kind:'evidence-lab',title:'Decode responsibly',prompt:'Sort the claims.',lanes:['Supported','Possible','Overreach'],items:['Revelation addresses seven churches.','Its imagery often echoes earlier biblical texts.','Every symbol has one obvious twenty-first-century referent.','Ignoring genre makes symbolic visions easier to interpret correctly.'],answer:[[0,0],[1,0],[2,2],[3,2]],hints:['Start with genre and original audience.'],why:'Revelation’s apocalyptic symbols require literary and scriptural context before modern application.'}},
'g.edges':{title:'Learn the shelf by its boundaries, not only by its middle',dek:'If you know where groups begin and end, a forgotten book becomes recoverable.',body:['Memory becomes more resilient when you know landmarks. Deuteronomy closes the Law. Joshua opens History. Esther closes History. Job opens Wisdom. Isaiah opens the Major Prophets. Daniel closes them. Hosea opens the Twelve. Malachi closes the Old Testament. Matthew opens the New Testament. Revelation closes it.','Boundaries are powerful because they tell you when the reading strategy and shelf neighborhood may change. Moving from Esther to Job is not simply “the next book”; the shelf shifts from historical narrative into wisdom/poetry. Moving from Malachi to Matthew crosses testaments and centuries of history.','Use boundary questions to self-correct. If you cannot remember what follows Esther, ask which group begins after History. If you cannot remember what precedes Matthew, ask which book closes the Protestant Old Testament.'],plain:'The edges of groups are landmarks. Learn them and the middle becomes easier to reconstruct.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Key shelf boundaries',nodes:[{title:'Deuteronomy → Joshua',text:'Law → History'},{title:'Esther → Job',text:'History → Wisdom'},{title:'Daniel → Hosea',text:'Major → Minor Prophets'},{title:'Malachi → Matthew',text:'Old → New Testament'},{title:'Jude → Revelation',text:'Letters → Apocalypse'}]},challenge:{kind:'match-board',title:'Match the boundary',prompt:'Connect each closing book with what comes next.',items:['Deuteronomy','Esther','Daniel','Malachi','Jude'],options:['Joshua','Job','Hosea','Matthew','Revelation'],answer:[0,1,2,3,4],hints:['Malachi crosses into Matthew.'],why:'Group boundaries are stable landmarks for whole-shelf navigation.'}},
'g.all':{title:'Nine shelf neighborhoods, one library',dek:'Whole-library fluency means knowing the groups while remembering that groups organize books rather than imprison them.',body:['A practical nine-neighborhood Protestant shelf map is Law, Historical Books, Poetry/Wisdom, Major Prophets, Minor Prophets, Gospels/Acts, Pauline Letters, General Letters, and Revelation. Some systems split Acts or Hebrews differently, but the navigation purpose remains the same.','Each neighborhood helps you predict broad questions. In Law, covenant and foundational narrative matter. In History, narrator and consequence matter. In Wisdom, literary form and generalization matter. In Prophets, historical crisis and rhetoric matter. In letters, sender, audience, and argument matter. In Revelation, apocalyptic symbolism matters.','But the map must stay flexible. A shelf group is not a cage. Daniel changes genre inside one book; letters quote poetry; Gospels contain parables and apocalyptic sayings. Mastery means using the map as an orientation tool and then zooming into the actual passage.'],plain:'Know the nine neighborhoods so you can orient quickly, then let the actual passage tell you what kind of reading it needs.',visual:{type:'shelf',title:'Nine neighborhoods of the Protestant shelf',books:[{title:'Law',group:0},{title:'History',group:1},{title:'Wisdom',group:2},{title:'Major Prophets',group:3},{title:'Minor Prophets',group:4},{title:'Gospels + Acts',group:5},{title:'Paul',group:6},{title:'General Letters',group:7},{title:'Revelation',group:8}],caption:'Navigation map, not a claim of nine pure literary genres.'},challenge:{kind:'capstone',title:'Choose the right first lens',prompt:'You open 1 Corinthians. What is the best first orientation?',clues:['It sits in the Pauline letters.','It addresses a named Christian community.','Its instructions respond to multiple reported problems and questions.'],options:['Treat every line as an isolated proverb','Read it as a letter with sender, audience, and argument','Read it as apocalyptic vision','Assume shelf group settles every interpretation'],answer:1,hints:['The book is correspondence.'],why:'Group knowledge should point you toward the book’s actual literary form and communicative situation.'}}
});
